  "articleBody": "Thinking about making your bathroom more senior-friendly? It's a smart move to plan ahead, and the bathroom is often a great place to start. A lot of folks wonder what changes are really necessary now versus what can wait, and how to make it safe without looking like a hospital room. The core idea is to balance safety with a design that still feels like home.\n\nFirst up, think about a curbless or roll-in shower. This is probably one of the biggest impact changes you can make for ease of access, especially if mobility becomes more challenging down the line. It really opens up the space and removes a tripping hazard. Next, grab bars are essential, but they don't have to be clinical. There are some really stylish options out there now that blend right in. Don't forget the toilet height – a raised toilet can make a big difference in comfort and ease of use. Also, consider non-slip flooring; there are many attractive options that provide better traction. Finally, ensure there's enough turning space, especially if a walker or wheelchair might be needed in the future. Planning these elements during a remodel is much easier and often more cost-effective than trying to retrofit them later. For larger bathroom remodels or whole-home changes, bringing in a professional can help coordinate everything safely.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
